      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The season's ending the way so many Blue Jays seasons do - with promise bruised by blunt reality and one last chance to leave a cleaner taste. <strong>Saturday's 5-1 loss to the Reds</strong> dropped Toronto to 78-83 and 5th in the AL East, and now the only drama left is Max Scherzer taking the ball in the finale, perhaps for the final time. Scherzer has said he'll make his retirement call later, in the offseason, which gives today's start a quiet but heavy gravity. The Jays have spent these final days sorting through a franchise-record 64 players, and that tells the story as plainly as the record does. This is not a club in chase mode. It's a club in audit mode. <strong>Watch Scherzer's start today - and then watch the young arms and bats get their final auditions before the winter plan begins.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated September 27, 2026"
      },
